Charles Joseph Minard, Carte figurative (1869)
----------------------------------------------

Datasets: army (the 40 stretches of the army's bands, drawn as bands as
wide as their men) and temperatures (the 8 temperatures of the retreat).

The work: Charles Joseph Minard (1781-1870), "Carte figurative des pertes
successives en hommes de l'Armée Française dans la campagne de Russie
1812-1813", Paris, 20 November 1869, lithograph by Regnier et Dourdet,
printed on one sheet with his chart of Hannibal's march. Minard died in
1870; the work is in the public domain everywhere.

Licence: recorded as CC-PDM-1.0, the Public Domain Mark, for a work free
of known copyright. https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/

Credit: After Charles Joseph Minard, 1869; scan: Bibliothèque nationale
de France.

The scan: the Bibliothèque nationale de France's, Gallica
btv1b52504201x (https://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/btv1b52504201x),
8,809 by 7,612 pixels at 400 dpi. Gallica refuses automated readers, so
the build reads the same scan from Wikimedia Commons, which holds it
with the source "Gallica btv1b52504201x" and the licence tag
PD-old-100-1923 (its author died more than 100 years ago and it was
published before 1923):
https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_figurative_des_pertes_successives_en_hommes_de_l%27arm%C3%A9e_fran%C3%A7aise_dans_la_Campagne_de_Russie_1812-13.jpg
read on 2026-10-12 at 12:11:37 UTC from

  https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Carte_figurative_des_pertes_successives_en_hommes_de_l%27arm%C3%A9e_fran%C3%A7aise_dans_la_Campagne_de_Russie_1812-13.jpg
  (6,504,247 bytes, SHA-256 219ce3632e0d5e6871032666d14d932e6321078f1f6c1cfdad233d728907a894)

The template carries no part of the scan: only the numbers and positions
read off it.

What was read, by us, on 2026-10-12, and kept in trace.json beside the
build (our own reading, released with this repository's templates):
- where Minard placed each of the 20 places he named;
- each band's centreline as he drew it, the advance (his red, here tan)
  and the retreat (black), the main army and the two parts sent north;
- the number of men on each stretch: the number Minard printed across the
  band; where he printed none (from Wilna to Witebsk, and on one stretch
  of the corps sent north to Polotzk), read from the band's width at his
  scale, one millimetre for 10,000 men, measured column by column on the
  scan and rounded to 5,000; and on one stretch, 340,000, his printed
  400,000 less his printed 60,000. Each stretch says which;
- his table of the retreat's temperatures, in degrees Réaumur below zero,
  with the dates he gave, each at the point of the retreat he set it
  under; Celsius added (Réaumur times 1.25).

Where his numbers and his widths disagree (from Smolensk to Dorogobouge
he printed 145,000 on a band drawn about 180,000 wide), the printed
number is kept.

The chart is laid on today's map with a thin-plate spline through the
20 places: each place sits at its Wikidata coordinate and the bands bend
smoothly between them, so the bands keep Minard's positions relative to
his places (his advance and retreat run apart where he drew them apart).
The bands are drawn at his scale on the ground, about 1.7 km for 10,000
men, with flat ends.


